| (iv) non-default settings kept | 5 / 150 | 5 / 150 |
| (v) `npm test` | 9 pass 0 fail | 4 pass 0 fail |
| (v) scoreboard in the client | yes | yes |

**The control did not route to UPGRADE.** On the shipped protocol, with `.bemyagent/` holding
only `settings.json` — 18.2's exact directory shape — q40 generated the scaffold and never wrote
an upgrade plan. No STOP was reached, so there was nothing to override.

That is **pre-mortem 1 happening**, written into `01_think.md` before launch: *"19.1's 6/6 came
from arms whose prompt is not recoverable, and the override may have been a product of that
wording rather than of the branch."* One arm is not a rate, and the remaining four run before
anything is concluded. But the shape to watch for is now visible: if the control does not take
the branch the clause was written to fix, **the clause has nothing to fix**, and that is a very
different verdict from the clause failing.

`override_language` returned one hit in q41 and none in q40. It greps the arm's own prose for
override wording and is **a weak proxy**, reported as such: a single word in a sentence about
something else looks identical to a real deviation. The routing artifacts are what decide (i).

